 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Non-timber forest products (NTFPs) are useful products collected from wild plants, without cutting the tree.\nExamples in Cameroon:\n• eru (okok): leaves of a forest vine, eaten as a vegetable (South West, Centre);\n• njangsa: seeds used as a spice;\n• bush mango (ndo'o, andok): seeds used to thicken soups;\n• bitter cola and kola nuts: chewed, used in ceremonies and medicine;\n• Pygeum (Prunus africana) bark: exported for medicine;\n• honey, mushrooms, rattan (for furniture), raffia, Gnetum, shea nuts and gum arabic in the North.\nMethods of collection: picking fallen fruits and nuts, cutting leaves, stripping bark (only part of it), tapping, collecting honey.\nImportance: food and medicine, income for rural families (especially women), trade and export, jobs.\nProblems: over-harvesting (uprooting eru, stripping all the bark kills the tree). Solutions: harvest sustainably and domesticate the plants (grow eru on farms).",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "p": "bush mango"
  },
  "problem": "How is {p} collected, and why is it important?",
  "steps": [
   "The ripe fruits fall; the seeds are removed, dried and sold.",
   "The seeds thicken soups, and their sale brings income to rural families."
  ],
  "answer": "Collected from fallen fruits; it gives food and income.",
  "checks": []
 },
